This section is written in the first person because the rubric asks what I learned, and the honest answer is a list of things I believed on Friday that were wrong by Sunday.

*The dataset design is the experiment.* I spent the first hours on tokenizers and architectures and almost none on how prompts were distributed across families. The first time I trained an input-only classifier it scored above chance and I briefly thought prompts carried a model signal. They did not; Bard had simply answered fewer Flan prompts. Building the controlled `uf` set (identical source quotas per family) and the `uf_natural` set side by side turned a confusing number into the clearest result in the report. Every later experiment inherited that discipline: grouped splits so prompts never leak, the same 1,200 prompts for every modern family, assertions in the build script rather than hopes. If I started over I would write `build.py` before `models.py`.

*A small CNN with random embeddings is a strong text classifier.* I expected DistilBERT to dominate and the CNN to be a baseline. DistilBERT won by two points on the five-family task, lost to the CNN on the 16-way task and on the 2026 families, at 16× the parameters and 50× the training time. The reason is in the ablations: the signal is local (openers, list markers, line breaks, hedges), and a max-pooled bank of width-3-to-5 filters is almost exactly the right inductive bias for "does this response contain any of these phrases". Pretraining helps with meaning; the fingerprint is not meaning.

*The LSTM needed design choices the CNN did not.* The textbook BiLSTM classifier reads the final hidden state. I pooled instead (masked max and mean over every time step, concatenated), because a fingerprint phrase at token 20 should not have to survive 480 more steps of recurrence to be counted, and that is the version reported here. Even so, the LSTM calibrates worst of all the models (ECE 0.16 against 0.08 for the CNN), is the least data-efficient on the learning curve, and gains the most from early stopping. The required comparison is therefore not "CNN vs RNN" so much as "a model whose inductive bias matches the signal vs one that has to learn to find it".

*Removing information can improve a classifier.* The function-words-only ablation, which deletes every content word, left the LSTM slightly better than full text. I checked it three times across seeds because it looked like a bug. It is not: content words add vocabulary the model can overfit to (prompt-specific nouns that appear in a handful of training responses), while the function-word skeleton is both lower-dimensional and where the style actually lives. This also explains why the input+output view is *worse* than output alone: more tokens, no more signal.

*Infrastructure is where the time went.* Generating the modern set was the slowest part of the project, not because of the 7,200 requests but because three of the first eight model endpoints were silently queued at under one response per minute and one never answered. Per-family worker processes with their own rate limits, resumable JSONL output and a shuffled prompt order (so that any prefix of the file is source-balanced) turned a stalled job into one that could be cut off at any moment and still yield a complete block design. Launching generation first and writing code while it ran was the single best scheduling decision of the weekend.

*Reproducibility is cheap if it is the default.* Every number in this PDF is written by `src/tables.py` from `results/*.json`; every figure by `src/plots.py`. When I found the input+output truncation bug on Friday evening (long ShareGPT prompts were pushing the response out of the window entirely), fixing it meant deleting the affected JSON files and rerunning one shell script. Nothing in the report had to be retyped. I will not write another project where results are copied by hand.

*What I would do with another week.* Temperature-scale the CNN and LSTM so their probabilities mean something; test whether a classifier trained on all 2023 and 2026 families jointly learns family-level features or just memorises each model; try a contrastive objective so that new models can be enrolled from a few examples without retraining; and run the paraphrase attack in both directions (paraphrase with each family and see whether the attribution follows the paraphraser).
